= Artificial intelligence =

== Overview ==
'''Artificial intelligence''' ('''AI''') is the field concerned with computational systems that perform tasks involving perception, reasoning, learning, planning, language, decision-making, action, and related capabilities associated with intelligent behavior.

AI is a broad field rather than one particular algorithm or model family.

== Major approaches ==
AI has included:

* search and problem solving;
* [[Knowledge representation|knowledge representation]];
* [[Reasoning|reasoning]] and inference;
* [[Planning (artificial intelligence)|planning]];
* [[Machine learning|machine learning]];
* perception and robotics;
* language processing;
* multi-agent systems;
* hybrid symbolic/statistical systems.

The relative importance of these approaches has changed over time.

== AI and machine learning ==
[[Machine learning]] is a major part of contemporary AI, but:

<pre>
artificial intelligence ≠ machine learning
</pre>

AI also includes systems whose behavior is primarily built from search, planning, logic, optimization, rules, or manually specified models.

== AI and human intelligence ==
AI systems can perform tasks associated with intelligent behavior without reproducing human cognition.

A system may be highly capable in one domain and weak in another.

Therefore task performance does not by itself establish human-like understanding, consciousness, or general intelligence.

== Agents ==
The agent perspective models intelligent systems as entities that perceive environments and choose actions.

[[Intelligent agent]] is the classical abstraction.

[[AI agent]] is a contemporary applied term for AI-powered systems that perform multi-step goal-directed work.

== Related concepts ==
[[Machine learning]], [[Reasoning]], [[Knowledge representation]], [[Planning (artificial intelligence)]], [[Intelligent agent]], [[Large language model]], and [[Perception]] provide major neighboring concepts.

